  "reference_class": "Annual changes in the US Treasury's end-of-fiscal-year average interest rate on total interest-bearing federal debt, using roughly the last 30 fiscal-year observations and weighting the last 10-15 years more heavily because the debt stock and maturity structure are more comparable.",
  "base_rate_reasoning": "This is a sticky stock-average that reprices gradually as debt matures. In the recent decade the series moved 2.35, 2.23, 2.28, 2.49, 2.49, 1.77, 1.61, 2.07, 2.97, 3.32. Even after a major rate shock, the annual increase slowed from +0.90 (FY2023) to +0.35 (FY2024). Historically, one-year moves larger than about 0.5 percentage points are uncommon because only a fraction of debt rolls over each year. The question itself points to a stable near-term regime: current level 3.32% at FY2024, CBO around 3.3-3.4% in nearby years, and only one fiscal year remains. That supports a modest rise from refinancing of older low-rate debt, but not a dramatic jump.",
