    "text": "Here are the relevant news articles:\n\n**Kenya's Sh13 Trillion Debt Bomb: Ruto Claims Stabilization as Treasury Data Shows 48% Increase**\nKenya's public debt reached Sh13.120.87 trillion as of June 2026, representing 68.5% of GDP and exceeding the 55% legal ceiling, according to the National Treasury's June 2026 Public Debt Bulletin. Domestic debt stood at Sh7,327.20 trillion, while external debt reached Sh5,793.66 trillion. President William Ruto claimed his administration slowed borrowing, citing a 27% increase in debt under his watch compared to three- and five-fold increases in previous administrations. However, Treasury data shows debt rose from Sh8.76 trillion in September 2022 to Sh13.120.87 trillion—a 48% increase. Controller of Budget Margaret Nyakang'o warned of rising refinancing needs and high debt-service costs, noting the Treasury spent Sh1.72 trillion in principal and interest payments in the 2024/25 financial year alone. The IMF has repeatedly flagged Kenya as having a high risk of debt distress; Communications Director Julie Kozack stated in November 2024 that 'any new borrowing should be considered within the context of a comprehensive fiscal strategy to reduce debt vulnerabilities.' Despite these warnings, Kenya secured $400 million in emergency financing from the World Bank and issued a $2.25 billion sovereign bond in February 2026 with interest rates of 7.9% and 8.7%. Efforts to secure a new IMF facility have stalled due to disputes over Sh335 billion in revenue-backed instruments not classified as public debt. With elections scheduled for August 2027 and debt service consuming approximately Sh2.6 trillion annually, analysts warn of a potential vicious cycle of borrowing.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 09:00 PM\nSource:[Standard Digital News - Kenya](https://www.standardmedia.co.ke/national/article/2001557708/inside-kenyas-sh13-trillion-debt-bomb-ruto-says-he-borrowed-less-treasury-data-says-otherwise)\n\n**US Debt Surpasses $40 Trillion, Threatening Global Economy and Israel**\nAccording to a report by the 'Jerusalem Post' cited by 'Sada al-Balad', US public debt has surpassed $40 trillion, creating a 'massive financial hole' that threatens to halt the global wave of interest rate cuts and impose heavy costs on other economies, including Israel. The US debt is growing at a rate of $7 billion daily, with net interest payments reaching approximately $970 billion in fiscal year 2025; the Congressional Budget Office (CBO) projects these payments will exceed $1 trillion for the first time this year, surpassing total defense spending, and could double to $2.1 trillion by 2036, representing a quarter of the federal budget. The International Monetary Fund (IMF) estimates US debt will exceed 140% of GDP by 2031. To finance the deficit, the US administration issued bonds worth $18.9 trillion from the start of 2026 through July, a 10.2% increase over the previous year, which drains global liquidity and pushes bond yields higher. Israel is already paying a price through three channels: higher interest rates increasing mortgage and loan installments; volatility on Wall Street increasing risks for Israeli retirement savings; and budget pressure forcing the state to spend billions of shekels on debt interest at the expense of education, health, and security. In a preventive measure, the regulatory body mandated financial institutions to hold a 10% foreign currency liquidity reserve. Experts like Jay Breminger of PricewaterhouseCoopers Israel warn that the technology and real estate sectors are most vulnerable, as high-risk assets lose funding when US Treasury bonds offer low-risk returns of 5%. Paul Marino of 'Themes ETFs' notes the dollar's reserve status gives the US a 'higher credit line,' while analyst Violeta Todorova of 'Leverage Shares' warns the risk lies in financing costs rather than default, as reduced foreign absorption could force the Treasury to raise yields in a vicious cycle. The Treasury has already doubled long-term bond buybacks from $2 billion to $4 billion per operation to curb yields, which reached 4.7% for 10-year bonds and 5.2% for 30-year bonds. Michael Peterson, CEO of the 'Peter G. Peterson Foundation', stated the US has run a deficit for 26 years, and every dollar paid in interest reduces citizen services and increases the likelihood of tax hikes. The report concludes that the current intervention is temporary and the final bill for the $40 trillion debt will be paid by consumers and companies worldwide through eroded wages, lower savings, and higher taxes.\nOriginal language: ar\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 12:54 PM\nSource:[صدى البلد](https://www.elbalad.news/7105671)\n\n**De La Espriella Government Faces Alarming Debt Levels with 2027 Budget Adjustment**\nThe government of Abelardo De La Espriella in Colombia has presented a budget adjustment for 2027 that includes a total of 534.9 trillion pesos, with a necessary increase in public debt due to a recalculated debt service of 37.4 trillion pesos. This adjustment raises the debt service from 118 trillion pesos (as estimated by the Gustavo Petro administration) to 155.4 trillion pesos, a move described by former Finance Minister Juan Camilo Restrepo as a realistic recognition of obligations rather than an increase in debt. The budget also incorporates previously unallocated funds for health, pensions, energy subsidies, public payroll, universities, and the Fuel Price Stabilization Fund (FEPC). Due to the decision not to implement new taxes, the government must rely on increased borrowing to cover the fiscal deficit. César Pabón of Corficolombiana notes that two-thirds of the budget increase stems from debt service, and the financing needs nearly doubled for 2027. Pabón emphasizes the importance of fiscal transparency but warns that the current fiscal rule (limiting debt to 71% of GDP) is inactive, and approaching 70% of GDP could pose stability risks without market confidence. The Autonomous Committee of the Fiscal Rule (Carf) urges the government to implement a convincing fiscal consolidation strategy to stabilize debt and return to the parametric rule. Restrepo warns that any new spending added during parliamentary debates will likely require additional debt issuance, as announced spending cuts (20.9 trillion pesos for this year and 40.2 trillion for 2027) have not yet been fully implemented. A major challenge is the high concentration of short-term debt, with Treasury Certificates (TCO) potentially exceeding 40 trillion pesos in the first half of 2027. To mitigate this, the Ministry of Finance is negotiating with creditors to refinance short-term obligations into longer-term instruments. José Ignacio López, president of the National Association of Financial Institutions (Anif), supports this approach but warns that long-term debt typically carries higher interest rates. He stresses that a clear message of austerity and the approval of the Economic Rescue Law in Congress are essential to secure lower long-term rates and avoid excessive refinancing costs.\nOriginal language: es\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 04:41 AM\nSource:[El Tiempo](https://www.eltiempo.com/economia/sectores/los-caminos-que-le-quedan-al-gobierno-de-la-espriella-para-enfrentar-una-deuda-cercana-a-niveles-alarmantes-3585566)\n\n**US Treasury Yields Surge Past 5.3% Amid Structural Shifts and Debt Concerns**\nOn July 31, the 30-year US Treasury yield surpassed 5.27%, its highest level since 2007, and rose above 5.3% in August, while the 10-year yield approached 5%. US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent attempted to lower yields by purchasing $6 billion in bonds, but the effort failed. Meanwhile, Donald Trump has been urging Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh, whom he appointed, to cut interest rates, even asking him to 'get smart.' However, it is highly likely that Warsh and the Fed governors will raise rates next Wednesday to curb inflation. The article attributes the rise in yields to structural forces reversing a 30-year trend, including Baby Boomers drawing down savings instead of accumulating them, a decline in Chinese foreign reserves from over $4,000 billion in 2014 to approximately $3,400 billion, and geopolitical risks such as the war in Iran and frozen Russian assets. Domestically, US federal debt exceeded $40,000 billion by the end of July, with interest payments reaching $857 billion in the first nine months of the fiscal year—a 13% increase from the previous year, surpassing spending on Medicare and defense. Additionally, demand for capital to fund AI data centers is driving up borrowing costs. According to Bloomberg Economics, the natural real rate on 10-year bonds reached 2.6% in the second quarter of 2026, consistent with nominal yields around 4.7%. Jim Reid of Deutsche Bank noted that long-term 10-year yields have averaged 4.5% since 1800, suggesting current levels are a 'normalization.' Consequently, long-term Treasury ETFs, such as those from iShares, have lost 4.4% in total return over the last year, impacting American households through higher mortgage, auto loan, and credit card rates.\nOriginal language: it\nPublish date: September 12, 2026 10:35 PM\nSource:[Il Messaggero](https://www.ilmessaggero.it/economia/news/bond_usa_rendimenti-9759769.html)\n\n**France's Financial Asphyxiation: Rising Rates and Debt Spiral**\nAccording to the article published in Challenges on September 12, 2026, France faces a financial crisis driven by surging interest rates and unsustainable public debt. The 10-year French government bond yield reached 4.45% on the Friday of publication, a level not seen since 2008, while the spread over German rates approaches 1 percentage point. France's public deficit stood at 5.1% of GDP in 2025, significantly higher than the Eurozone average of 2.9%. The article explains that a sustained 1 percentage point increase in rates adds approximately 3.2 billion euros to debt servicing costs in the first year, rising to 23.5 billion euros after five years and 33.5 billion euros after nine years. The 'snowball effect' is described as a cycle where higher interest rates lead to larger deficits, requiring more debt issuance, which in turn drives rates even higher. Total mandatory levies on companies reached 424.5 billion euros in 2025, representing 42.6 points of GDP, compared to a European average of 7.3 points. Economic growth is projected to remain sluggish, with the Insee forecasting only 0.4% growth for the year, while the government revised its 2026 budget growth assumption down to 0.5%. The article concludes that the 2027 presidential election will hinge on candidates' willingness to cut spending and stimulate growth rather than promising debt cancellation.\nOriginal language: fr\nPublish date: September 12, 2026 10:30 AM\nSource:[Challenges](https://www.challenges.fr/economie/lasphyxie-financiere-cest-maintenant-sortir-de-limplacable-mecanique-de-la-dette-un-imperatif_645726)\n\n**Post-Summer Debt Crisis: Statistics and Steps to Manage Financial Dread**\nAccording to Vicky Parry in the Mirror, families are facing a 'post-summer debt' crisis as school costs and credit card bills from summer spending converge. According to the Bank of England's Money and Credit report for July 2026, households took on a net £2 billion in consumer credit during the month, with consumer credit growing at 9.2% and credit-card borrowing growing at 12.5%, while the effective interest rate on interest-charging credit cards was 21.45%. StepChange's 2025 Statistics Yearbook, published in March 2026, shows the average total of arrears and unsecured debt among its new clients rose by 10%, from £17,936 in 2024 to £19,701 in 2025, with six in ten of its new clients in employment. Ministry of Housing, Communities and Local Government figures show that the cumulative amount of council tax outstanding in England reached £7.4 billion at the end of March 2026, which was £782 million higher than a year earlier. Vikki Brownridge, chief executive of StepChange, stated that 'With one in three of those coming to StepChange behind on this bill, these latest figures are no surprise to us.' Parry advises individuals to open all statements, create a budget, prioritize essential bills like rent and council tax, and contact lenders to negotiate support. She also recommends checking for unclaimed benefits, pausing unused subscriptions, and seeking free help from organizations like MoneyHelper, StepChange, National Debtline, and Citizens Advice.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 12, 2026 06:06 AM\nSource:[Mirror](https://www.mirror.co.uk/money/post-summer-debt-hitting-families-37649213)\n\n**US Treasury Yields Surge Past 5.3% Amid Structural Shifts and Debt Concerns**\nOn July 31, the 30-year US Treasury yield surpassed 5.27%, its highest level since 2007, and rose above 5.3% in August, while the 10-year yield approached 5%. US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent attempted to lower yields by purchasing $6 billion in bonds, but the effort failed. Meanwhile, Donald Trump has been urging Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh, whom he appointed, to cut interest rates, even asking him to 'get smart.' However, it is highly likely that Warsh and the Fed governors will raise rates next Wednesday to curb inflation. The article attributes the rise in yields to structural forces reversing a 30-year trend, including Baby Boomers drawing down savings instead of accumulating them, a decline in Chinese foreign reserves from over $4,000 billion in 2014 to approximately $3,400 billion, and geopolitical risks such as the war in Iran and frozen Russian assets. Domestically, US federal debt exceeded $40,000 billion by the end of July, with interest payments reaching $857 billion in the first nine months of the fiscal year—a 13% increase from the previous year, surpassing spending on Medicare and defense. Additionally, demand for capital to fund AI data centers is driving up borrowing costs. According to Bloomberg Economics, the natural real rate on 10-year bonds reached 2.6% in the second quarter of 2026, consistent with nominal yields around 4.7%. Jim Reid of Deutsche Bank noted that long-term 10-year yields have averaged 4.5% since 1800, suggesting current levels are a 'normalization.' Consequently, long-term Treasury ETFs, such as those from iShares, have lost 4.4% in total return over the last year, impacting American households through higher mortgage, auto loan, and credit card rates.\nOriginal language: it\nPublish date: September 12, 2026 10:35 PM\nSource:[Il Messaggero](https://www.ilmessaggero.it/economia/news/bond_usa_rendimenti-9759769.html)\n\n**France's Financial Asphyxiation: Rising Rates and Debt Spiral**\nAccording to the article published in Challenges on September 12, 2026, France faces a financial crisis driven by surging interest rates and unsustainable public debt. The 10-year French government bond yield reached 4.45% on the Friday of publication, a level not seen since 2008, while the spread over German rates approaches 1 percentage point. France's public deficit stood at 5.1% of GDP in 2025, significantly higher than the Eurozone average of 2.9%. The article explains that a sustained 1 percentage point increase in rates adds approximately 3.2 billion euros to debt servicing costs in the first year, rising to 23.5 billion euros after five years and 33.5 billion euros after nine years. The 'snowball effect' is described as a cycle where higher interest rates lead to larger deficits, requiring more debt issuance, which in turn drives rates even higher. Total mandatory levies on companies reached 424.5 billion euros in 2025, representing 42.6 points of GDP, compared to a European average of 7.3 points. Economic growth is projected to remain sluggish, with the Insee forecasting only 0.4% growth for the year, while the government revised its 2026 budget growth assumption down to 0.5%. The article concludes that the 2027 presidential election will hinge on candidates' willingness to cut spending and stimulate growth rather than promising debt cancellation.\nOriginal language: fr\nPublish date: September 12, 2026 10:30 AM\nSource:[Challenges](https://www.challenges.fr/economie/lasphyxie-financiere-cest-maintenant-sortir-de-limplacable-mecanique-de-la-dette-un-imperatif_645726)\n\n**Global Bond Yields Rise as Markets Force Governments to Confront Debt Costs**\nGlobal bond markets are signaling a shift in investor sentiment, driving up government borrowing costs as investors demand higher yields to compensate for large debts, persistent deficits, and above-target inflation. According to data from Trading Economics as of September 2, 2025, the United States 10-year Treasury yield is 4.7850%, the United Kingdom's is 5.2255%, and Japan's has risen to 3.0180%, while China's yield has dropped to 1.6860%. The article explains that governments entered the pandemic heavily indebted and responded with massive fiscal programs, but subsequent inflation has forced central banks to maintain restrictive monetary policy. For the United States, gross federal debt recently surpassed $40 trillion, with roughly $32.3 trillion held by the public. The article notes that the average interest rate on interest-bearing Treasury debt rose from 3.32% in January to roughly 3.45% by July. The American Institute for Economic Research calculates that every sustained 5-basis-point increase in financing costs on the $32.3 trillion debt base results in approximately $16 billion more in annual interest expense. The US Congressional Budget Office (CBO) projects net federal interest expense will exceed $1 trillion in fiscal 2026, surpassing the roughly $898 billion defense budget. The article argues that rising yields are forcing governments to confront the reality that money spent servicing debt cannot be spent on other priorities like defense, healthcare, or Social Security.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 03, 2026 07:05 AM\nSource:[American Institute for Economic Research](https://thedailyeconomy.org/article/bond-markets-are-losing-patience-and-putting-governments-on-notice/)\n\n**US Public Debt Surpasses $40 Trillion Amid Rising Interest Rates and Fiscal Concerns**\nOn August 19, 2026, the public debt of the United States surpassed 40 trillion dollars (specifically $40,470,470,230,213), having more than doubled in value between 2016 and 2026, with a recent annual increase of 3 trillion dollars. According to market data, the 10-year Treasury bond rate reached 4.71 percent, the highest since 2007, while the 30-year rate hit 5.26 percent, its highest level in 19 years. The International Bourse (BIS) states that the probability of fiscal consolidation is nearly zero, noting that historical market manias often follow technological booms with capital excess, eventually leading to investment reversals and recessions. Currently, 80 percent of the debt is held by domestic agents including the Federal Reserve, foreign governments, pension funds, and private investors, while the remaining 20 percent is owed to federal agencies like Social Security. Approximately one-quarter of the debt is held by international investors and foreign governments such as Japan, the United Kingdom, and China. The estimated interest payment for this fiscal year exceeds 1 trillion dollars at an average rate of 3.5 percent, with a daily interest cost of approximately 3.3 billion dollars, resulting in a debt-to-GDP ratio of 123 percent. The article notes that aggressive fiscal policies and the economic impact of the war in Iran and rising oil prices have contributed to this scenario, while a recent attempt by the Treasury to buy back long-term bonds to lower yields failed to achieve its objective.\nOriginal language: es\nPublish date: August 24, 2026 12:00 AM\nSource:[La Jornada](https://www.jornada.com.mx/noticia/2026/08/24/opinion/deuda-publica-y-el-dolar)\n\n**US Deficit Explodes to $1.8 Trillion as Interest Costs Surge**\nAccording to data published by Investireoggi.it on August 14, 2026, the US federal deficit has reached record levels, with a monthly deficit of $432 billion in July, a sharp increase from $291 billion the previous year. For the first 10 months of the fiscal year (October 2025–July 2026), the cumulative deficit has risen to $1.8 trillion, a 5% year-over-year increase, with full-year estimates now projected at $2.1 trillion. Revenue fell from $338 billion to $334 billion, attributed to the Supreme Court-ordered return of tariffs. Federal spending for the period totaled $6.284 trillion against revenues of $4.485 trillion, pushing the national debt close to $40 trillion. The composition of spending shows Social Security at $1.38 trillion, Net Interest on Debt at $963 billion (surpassing the $804 billion defense budget), Medicare at $955 billion, and Medicaid/Health at $855 billion. The average interest rate paid on Treasuries is currently 3.41%, though the weighted average yield on markets is around 4.50%. The 30-year Treasury yield has stabilized above 5.20%, its highest level since 2007. Inflation stood at 3.4% in July, remaining above the 2% target for the 65th consecutive month. The article notes that over the decade 2016–2025, the federal debt grew by over $19.2 trillion (+100%), while GDP grew by less than $12.5 trillion (+68%), meaning each dollar of new debt generated only 65 cents of nominal growth, compared to 1.57 dollars of growth per dollar of debt in the decade prior to the 2008 financial crisis.\nOriginal language: it\nPublish date: August 14, 2026 07:02 AM\nSource:[Investireoggi.it](https://www.investireoggi.it/deficit-usa-1800-miliardi-conto-interessi-esplode/)\n\n**US Daily Interest Payments Surpass $3 Billion as Debt Costs Soar**\nAccording to calculations by the US Congressional Budget Office (CBO), the US federal government spent approximately $963 billion in net interest on public debt between October and July of the 2026 fiscal year, a figure that is $117 billion higher than the same period last year, representing a 14% year-over-year increase. Consequently, the daily average net interest expense for the US Treasury has exceeded $3 billion, bringing the total interest payments for the fiscal year close to the $1 trillion mark before the year ends. Additionally, the US federal budget deficit reached approximately $1.8 trillion in the first 10 months of the fiscal year, an increase of $169 billion compared to the previous year. While federal revenues grew by approximately 3% annually, expenditures rose by 5%, creating a cycle where high deficits necessitate more borrowing, which in turn increases interest costs. The article notes that even if market interest rates fall, the budget will not see immediate relief because a large portion of the debt stock must be refinanced at higher rates over time, squeezing the US government's ability to fund other areas.\nOriginal language: tr\nPublish date: August 11, 2026 11:43 AM\nSource:[Dünya](https://www.dunya.com/ekonomi/abd-her-gun-3-milyar-dolari-faize-oduyor-haberi-835783)\n\n**NTMA Warns Ireland's National Debt Interest Costs to Double by 2030**\nThe National Treasury Management Agency (NTMA) of Ireland projects that the cost of servicing the national debt's interest bill will double from €3 billion to €6 billion by 2030, as the era of record low interest rates ends. Frank O'Connor, the NTMA's chief executive, stated that while the stock of debt is projected to rise to nearly €250 billion (up from €210 billion), Ireland remains in a 'strong position' to manage these costs, though the country's status as a small, open economy means this cannot be taken for granted. Last year, the NTMA borrowed €8.5 billion at an average interest rate of 3.08%, with over a-third raised via 30-year bonds yielding 3.15%; the average interest rate on the total debt stock remained stable at 1.5%. The national debt fell for the fourth consecutive year to €210 billion, and Ireland holds one of the longest debt maturities in Europe. So far in 2026, €8.25 billion has been borrowed, with plans to borrow between €10 billion and €14 billion in 2026. Additionally, two long-term savings funds managed by the NTMA for infrastructure are on track to exceed €23 billion by year-end, having earned a 2.2% investment return last year under a low-risk strategy, despite criticism from the Irish Fiscal Advisory Council and Central Bank Governor Gabriel Makhlouf regarding borrowing to fund these payments.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: July 08, 2026 01:00 PM\nSource:[RTE.ie](https://www.rte.ie/news/business/2026/0708/1582391-national-treasury-management-agency-annual-report/)\n\n**Panama's Public Debt Surges $1.9bn in April Amid Fiscal Deficit Concerns**\nPanama's public debt surged by $1,888mn in April 2026, bringing the total to $61,896mn, a 3.1% monthly increase confirmed by the Directorate of Public Financing at the Ministry of Economy and Finance (MEF). This jump contrasts sharply with the first quarter 2026 average of $253mn monthly growth. Finance Minister Felipe Chapman has not responded to press enquiries regarding the cause. According to official figures, gross disbursements and capitalisations exceeded $3,120mn while capital repayments were roughly $1,416mn, resulting in a net increase despite exchange-rate adjustments. New borrowings included $2,500mn from international banks, $500mn from the state-owned Banco Nacional de Panamá, and $109mn in Treasury Bills, with interest payments reaching $189mn. Ernesto Bazán, a risk analyst and economist quoted by La Estrella de Panamá, described the spike as an 'accounting hiccup' driven by deferred spending from 2019, but cautioned that structural overspending ensures continued borrowing. Panama recorded a fiscal deficit of $1,669mn in Q1 2026, with the 2026 budget committing to $8,000mn in debt repayments. Rolando Gordón, dean of the Faculty of Economics at the University of Panama, noted the country is borrowing to repay prior loans. External debt accounts for $51,877mn (over 80%), with global bonds at $31,096mn and direct bank loans rising from $7,354mn in March to $10,022mn in April following agreements with institutions including Citibank. The government frames this shift as diversification away from bond markets due to elevated global interest rates. Raúl Moreira, economist and former head of economic analysis at the MEF, stated that while debt growth will not stop, its rate is not yet under control. Chapman acknowledged a need for approximately $1,000mn in additional financing before year-end to meet 2026 targets. Analysts warn that Panama's tax revenue of 13-14 cents per dollar of economic output—roughly half the Latin American average—leaves minimal fiscal space, and the debt stock has more than doubled since the start of the Cortizo administration in 2019.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: May 27, 2026 07:21 PM\nSource:[bne IntelliNews](https://www.intellinews.com/panama-s-public-debt-surges-1-9bn-in-a-single-month-raising-fiscal-alarm-445214/)\n\n**Mortgage Rates Surge to 6.75% as Borrowing Costs Climb Amid Inflation and Debt Concerns**\nAccording to data from Mortgage News Daily, the average U.S. 30-year fixed mortgage rate climbed to 6.75% on Tuesday, the highest level since July 2025, prompting warnings from market commentator The Kobeissi Letter. The Kobeissi Letter, in a thread on X published May 19, 2026, stated that 'inflation is simply too hot' and warned that 'the average rate on these mortgages will cross above 7.00% soon.' The account highlighted that rates had surged from below 6% before the Iran War to nearly 6.75% this week, jumping 33 basis points in just 10 days. This increase has affected affordability, with monthly payments on a median-priced $420,000 home rising by roughly $167 compared with earlier rate levels. Shares of major homebuilders also declined on Tuesday, with D.R. Horton, Inc. (NYSE:DHI) falling 2.01%, Lennar Corporation (NYSE:LEN) slipping 0.98%, and PulteGroup, Inc. (NYSE:PHM) losing 0.65%. The Kobeissi Letter tied the surge to mounting pressure in the Treasury and bond markets, citing a U.S. budget deficit of $1.2 trillion during the first six months of fiscal 2026 and total U.S. debt climbing to a record $39 trillion. The account noted that markets entered 2026 expecting four Federal Reserve rate cuts, but traders are now pricing in future rate hikes, and the 10-year Treasury yield has climbed above levels seen during President Donald Trump's tariff-related market volatility in 2025.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: May 20, 2026 11:13 AM\nSource:[Benzinga](https://www.benzinga.com/real-estate/26/05/52685821/will-mortgage-rates-hit-7-as-borrowing-costs-keep-climbing)\n\n**U.S. National Debt Surpasses $39 Trillion in March 2026 Amid Rising Interest Rates and Foreign Shifts**\nAs of March 2026, the total U.S. national debt has surpassed $39 trillion, increasing by $2.77 trillion over the past year and rising at a rate of approximately $86,300 per second, according to data from 'Araam Financial Gate' (بوابة أرقام المالية). The debt burden per American household has reached $289,200, while the per capita share is approximately $114,000. The composition of the debt includes $31.4 trillion in public debt held by investors (individuals, institutions, and foreign governments), and $7.6 trillion in intragovernmental holdings, primarily held by trust funds like 'Social Security'. The Federal Reserve is the largest single creditor, holding $4.4 trillion in debt, which exceeds the combined holdings of the top three foreign creditors. Domestic investors hold the largest share at $17.7 trillion. Among foreign creditors, Japan remains the largest holder with $1.2 trillion, while China's holdings have declined to $683 billion in 2025 from $1.26 trillion in 2015. The United Kingdom's holdings surged to $889 billion in 2025 from $205 billion in 2015, and the Cayman Islands hold $427 billion. In March 2026, the average interest rate on tradable debt reached 3.365%, up from 1.499% five years prior. The Congressional Budget Office projects that interest payments will consume 13.95% of total federal spending in 2026, potentially reducing funding for infrastructure and defense. The article notes that these figures were recorded shortly after the launch of U.S. military operations against Iran.\nOriginal language: ar\nPublish date: April 20, 2026 03:35 PM\nSource:[بوابة أرقام المالية](https://www.argaam.com/ar/article/articledetail/id/1898011)\n\n"
