  "da_critique": "Strongest case the team forecast is too low.\n\n1. They appear anchored to weak, secondhand snapshots instead of the likely resolving series.\nThe core official series is Treasury Fiscal Data’s `avg_interest_rate_amt` for “Interest-bearing Debt / Total Interest-bearing Debt” (E1, E10), but the forecast leans heavily on media summaries and third-party articles for 2026 levels (E12–E15, E17–E19). Those sources cite “average cost,” “tradable debt,” or broad debt-service numbers, which are not necessarily the exact resolving field. If those third-party figures are already saying ~3.41–3.45% by July–August 2026 (E12, E13, E15), then a Sep 30 resolving value with median only 3.53% may understate the risk that the official value is already at or above the upper half of the interval.\n\n2. The forecast may be underestimating the late-2026 passthrough from market rates to the stock average.\nThe team includes evidence that Treasury market yields were elevated very late in the window: 30Y above 5.27% and 10Y near 5% on 2026-07-31 (E18), with daily nominal and real yield data available through 2026-09-11 showing the period remained current and relevant (E7, E8). Since the resolving metric is an average rate on total unmatured interest-bearing debt (E1), every maturing low-coupon security refinanced in FY2026 tends to raise the stock average when replacement coupons are near 4.5–5.5%. With only about two months between the July 2026 official schedule and fiscal year-end, the direction is mechanically upward unless issuance composition becomes unusually favorable. The team’s spread implies only modest additional increase from the cited July/August level to Sep 30.\n\n3. They may be ignoring a composition effect that pushes the average up faster than expected.\nEvidence E2 shows FRNs had a 5.0% average rate as of 2024-09-30, while TIPS were only 0.6%. Floating-rate debt reprices quickly, unlike fixed coupon debt. In a high short-rate environment, FRNs raise the aggregate average faster than legacy notes/bonds alone would. If FY2026 issuance or outstanding mix still includes meaningful FRN exposure, the aggregate rate can keep climbing even without huge changes in long-end yields. The team’s median near 3.53% may not fully capture this repricing channel, especially given elevated rates in E18.\n\n4. Debt growth itself is not neutral if financed at current rates.\nThe forecast may implicitly treat the average rate as moving slowly because of the large inherited stock, but E16, E17, and E19 show debt above $40T by late FY2026 with rapidly rising interest outlays. New borrowing to fund deficits is issued at current market rates, which are well above much of the legacy book. Rapid net issuance at high rates can pull the weighted-average rate upward faster than simple “old debt rolls off slowly” heuristics imply. If debt is expanding materially during the final months, the marginal debt’s high coupon has more weight in the total.\n\n5. The official July 2025 intragovernmental figure may be causing false comfort.\nE4 says intragovernmental debt holdings without TIPS and FRNs had an average interest rate of 3.1% as of 2025-07-31. But that is not the resolving series and excludes the categories most relevant for rapid repricing. It also predates the resolution date by 14 months. Using it as a stability anchor risks understating how much the total interest-bearing average can rise by Sep 2026. Likewise E3 is about debt held by the public and debt-category tables, not necessarily the precise resolving field.\n\n6. There may be an “already-resolved” smell here that argues for a higher point estimate.\nThe team notes the latest retrievable official schedule is 2025-07-31 and that no 2026-09-30 page was found in search (E5, E6), but they do have multiple contemporaneous 2026 reports clustered around 3.41–3.45 by March–August (E12–E15). If several independent media pieces are all quoting roughly the same current average debt cost by mid-August, then by end-September the official value is unlikely to sit near the low tail around 3.30–3.40 absent a sharp reversal—which cannot happen quickly for a stock average. The lower half of the forecast looks too generous.\n\n7. The uncertainty band may be too wide on the downside and too narrow on the upside.\nGiven the stock-average nature of the metric, downside surprises are limited over a 1–2 month horizon from already-reported ~3.45 levels (E12, E15). Upside surprises remain plausible if August/September refinancing occurred at elevated rates (E18) and if large net issuance continued (E16, E19). So the asymmetry is upward, not symmetric around 3.53.\n\nBest directional critique: move the forecast upward by about 0.10–0.20 percentage points, with the median more like 3.62–3.70 rather than 3.53."
